Order of Precedence of Logical Operators
When multiple logical operators are used in a single expression, Python follows operator precedence to determine the order in which operators are evaluated.

The and operator has higher precedence than or, so and is evaluated first unless parentheses specify a different order.
Within expressions of the same precedence, Python evaluates conditions from left to right and applies short-circuit evaluation.




def check(x):
    print("Method called for value:", x)
    return x > 0
​
a = check
b = check
c = check
​
if a(-1) or b(5) or c(10):
    print("At least one of the numbers is positive")

Output
Method called for value: -1
Method called for value: 5
Atleast one of the number is positive
Explanation:

a(-1) returns False, so Python checks the next condition.
b(5) returns True, so Python stops evaluating further conditions.
c(10) is never executed due to short-circuit behavior of or.
